**Quick answer:** To get a remote developer job in India, decide which type you want (a remote role at an Indian company, or a role with a global company hiring from India), search on job boards that list direct company postings, tailor your resume to remote work, show proof of async communication skills, and prepare for the legal and payment setup, which can be employment, contractor, or Employer of Record (EOR). Apply early, because remote roles attract large applicant pools.

Remote work has opened a path for Indian developers to work with teams across the world without relocating. It has also made the market more competitive. This guide covers where to look, how to apply, what to know about contracts and payments, and how to stand out.

## Two kinds of remote developer jobs for people in India

Before you search, understand which category you are aiming for. The application process, pay, and legal setup differ.

### 1. Remote roles at Indian companies

These companies are based in India and hire people to work from home or from anywhere in the country. You are typically a regular employee under Indian labor and tax rules, paid in rupees. Some companies are fully remote, and others are hybrid with occasional office visits.

### 2. Global remote roles open to candidates in India

These are roles at companies abroad that hire from India. Postings often say "Remote (India)," "Remote (APAC)," "Remote (worldwide)," or list a time zone range. How you are engaged varies:

- **Direct employment** through a local entity the company has set up.
- **Employer of Record (EOR)**, where a third-party provider legally employs you on behalf of the company.
- **Independent contractor**, where you invoice the company and handle your own taxes.

Some global roles require overlap with US, UK, or European working hours. Read the time zone expectations carefully.

## Where to find remote developer jobs

### Use job boards that show direct company postings

Many good roles are posted directly on company career pages through applicant tracking systems such as Greenhouse, Workday, and Workable. Searching those directly is hard because there are thousands of companies. A job board that gathers them in one place saves time.

On GetHired you can start with [remote jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?location=Remote) and [jobs in India](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?location=India), or browse by city:

- [Remote jobs in Bengaluru](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/remote-jobs/Bengaluru)
- [Remote jobs in Hyderabad](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/remote-jobs/hyderabad)
- [Remote jobs in Pune](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/remote-jobs/pune)
- [Remote jobs in Gurugram](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/remote-jobs/gurgaon)
- [Remote jobs in Chennai](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/remote-jobs/chennai)

You can also use AI search to type a plain-language request such as "remote React developer jobs" instead of guessing keywords. Our [guide to the best remote job platforms](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-best-platforms-to-find-remote-jobs-in-2026-your-guide-to-career-freedom) compares more places to look.

### Other places worth checking

- **Company career pages** for businesses you already admire.
- **Developer communities** such as Discord servers, Slack groups, and open-source project spaces where hiring posts appear.
- **LinkedIn and professional networks**, for referrals and recruiter outreach.
- **Startup communities and funded-company lists**, covered in our guide on [getting a job at a funded startup](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-get-a-job-at-a-funded-startup-in-2026).

### Set up job alerts

Remote roles can receive hundreds of applications within days. Job alerts help you see new postings quickly so you can apply early. Alerts based on your skills and preferred salary range keep your pipeline full without daily manual searching.

## Which developer roles are commonly remote?

Remote openings appear across engineering, but some roles are more common than others. Typical examples include:

- Frontend developer (React, Vue, Angular, Next.js)
- Backend developer (Node.js, Python, Java, Go)
- Full-stack developer
- Mobile developer (React Native, Flutter, Swift, Kotlin)
- DevOps and cloud engineer
- Data engineer and machine learning engineer
- QA and test automation engineer

Seniority matters. Many global companies hire mid-level and senior engineers for fully remote work, while junior remote roles are fewer and more competitive. If you are early in your career, a strong portfolio and open-source contributions matter a lot. Our guide to a [standout portfolio](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-definitive-guide-to-crafting-a-standout-portfolio) can help.

## How to apply for remote developer jobs

### Step 1: Write your resume for remote work

Remote hiring managers look for evidence that you can work independently. Include:

- Projects you owned from start to finish
- Experience with distributed teams or different time zones
- Tools for async work, such as Slack, Notion, Jira, Linear, GitHub, and Loom
- Clear, measurable outcomes
- A line that states your location and time zone availability

Make sure your resume is readable by applicant tracking systems. See our guide on [how to check your resume for ATS compatibility](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-check-if-your-resume-will-pass-an-ats-in-2026-and-fix-it-in-5-minutes) and our post on [optimizing your resume for remote jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-optimize-your-resume-for-remote-jobs-a-guide-for-modern-professionals).

### Step 2: Tailor each application

Read the posting for the tech stack, time zone, and work authorization requirements. Mirror the relevant terms where they honestly apply. A short, specific cover letter that mentions the company's product or engineering challenges is better than a generic one.

### Step 3: Show your work

Remote managers cannot watch you work, so they rely on visible proof:

- A GitHub profile with readable code and clear READMEs
- A portfolio with live links
- Technical blog posts or documentation you wrote
- Open-source contributions

### Step 4: Apply early and track everything

Apply soon after a posting goes live, and keep a tracker with the company, role, date, link, and status. Our [step-by-step ATS application guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-apply-to-jobs-on-greenhouse-workday-lever-and-workable-a-step-by-step-guide) shows how to move through Greenhouse, Workday, Lever, and Workable forms efficiently.

### Step 5: Prepare for remote-specific interviews

Expect a mix of technical and communication-focused rounds. Interviewers often test:

- **Written communication**, through take-home tasks or clear emails
- **Self-management**, with questions about how you prioritize and unblock yourself
- **Collaboration across time zones**
- **Technical depth**, through coding rounds, system design, and past project discussions

Practice out loud with the [GetHired AI Interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/ai-interview), and see our guide on [the art of the remote interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-art-of-the-remote-interview-from-preparation-to-follow-up).

## Contracts, taxes, and payments: what to understand

This part is where many Indian developers get surprised. The details depend on your situation, so treat the points below as a starting checklist and confirm specifics with a qualified chartered accountant or tax professional.

- **Employment type matters.** Employee, EOR, and contractor arrangements carry different tax treatment, benefits, and protections.
- **Ask how you will be paid.** Common methods include bank transfers through the EOR or payroll provider, and international payment platforms for contractors.
- **Understand currency and conversion costs.** Fees and exchange rates can reduce what you actually receive.
- **Check benefits.** Contractors often do not receive paid leave, health insurance, or provident fund contributions unless negotiated.
- **Read the contract.** Look at termination terms, notice periods, intellectual property clauses, and non-compete language.
- **Keep records.** Maintain invoices, contracts, and payment proofs for tax filing.
- **Watch for scams.** Be cautious of any employer that asks you to pay a fee, buy equipment upfront from them, or move conversations to unofficial channels before a formal offer.

## Time zones and working hours

Remote does not always mean flexible. Many global roles expect some overlap with the team's hours. For a developer in India, that can mean working late evenings for US teams, or early mornings for some US and European schedules. Ask during the interview:

- What are the core overlap hours?
- Are meetings recorded, and is the team async-first?
- How often are on-call or support shifts expected?

Teams that are truly async-first tend to offer more flexibility, so look for those signals in the job description.

## How to stand out from the competition

Remote postings attract applicants from many countries. A few things help you rise above the pile.

1. **Be specific.** "Built a Next.js dashboard that cut report load time from 8 seconds to 2" beats "Worked on dashboards."
1. **Communicate clearly in writing.** Your application is a sample of how you will communicate on the job.
1. **Apply to fewer, better-matched roles** instead of firing off hundreds of identical applications.
1. **Build in public.** Share projects, write about what you learn, and contribute to open source.
1. **Know the company.** Mention a product feature, engineering blog post, or recent launch.
1. **Respond fast.** Reply to recruiters and scheduling emails promptly.
1. **Follow up politely** after a week or two if you have not heard back.

## Common mistakes to avoid

- Applying with a resume that does not parse well in an ATS
- Ignoring time zone and work authorization requirements in the posting
- Sending the same cover letter to every company
- Not asking about payment method and contract type until the end
- Skipping practice for async communication and take-home assignments

## Frequently asked questions

### Can I get a remote developer job from India with a global company?

Yes. Many global companies hire developers in India, especially for mid-level and senior roles. Look for postings labeled "Remote (India)," "Remote (APAC)," or "Remote (worldwide)," and check time zone and work authorization requirements.

### Do remote developer jobs in India pay in rupees or dollars?

It depends on the employer. Indian companies usually pay in rupees. Global companies may pay in dollars or another currency, often through an EOR or as a contractor payment. Confirm the currency and payment method before accepting.

### What is an Employer of Record (EOR)?

An EOR is a third-party company that legally employs you on behalf of a foreign company. It handles payroll, taxes, and compliance locally, while you do the day-to-day work for the client company.

### Are there remote jobs for freshers and junior developers?

Yes, but there are fewer of them and competition is high. A strong portfolio, internships, open-source work, and clear communication can help you compete.

### Which Indian cities have the most remote developer jobs?

Remote roles are location-flexible, but Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Pune, Gurugram, and Chennai are common hubs for tech hiring. You can browse listings for each on GetHired's city remote jobs pages.

### How do I avoid remote job scams?

Never pay a fee to get a job, be wary of offers that come with no interview, verify the company through its official website, and confirm that recruiters use official email domains.

### How many hours of overlap do remote jobs usually require?

It varies. Some teams are fully async, while others require several hours of overlap with their local working hours. Ask about this early in the process.

